  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 PM EST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nAfternoon GOES satellite imagery reveals a low amplitude shortwave \ndigging through northern MN with it's main isentropic ascent region \noccurring across the UP. Thus far, the moisture starved shortwave \nhasn't been able to produce anything other than cloud cover. Can't \nrule out a few sprinkles across the northern tier of the UP this \nafternoon, but no measurable precip has been added to the forecast. \nTemps in the 40s to low 50s fall back to the 30s to near 40, warmest \nlakeshores. The shortwave kicks east through Ontario tonight, \nveering breezy winds to the NW through Tuesday and keeping dry \nconditions in place with similar temps expected.\n\nThe more impactful forecast challenge presents itself during the mid-\nto-late week through early this weekend. A shortwave slides south of \nthe UP early wednesday, touching off light rain across the UP. At \nthe same time, troughing in eastern Canada retrogrades through \nQuebec and eastern Ontario, sending a parade of additional \nshortwaves across the Upper Great Lakes while spilling cold air \naloft across the region. 850 mb temps late Wednesday begin to fall \nbelow -5C and hover between -7 to -11C over a ~+8C Lake Superior sfc \ntemp, inducing lake effect snow showers across the N to NNE wind \nsnowbelts across the region through at least early Saturday. There \nremains some uncertainty surrounding where the highest snowfall \ntotals will setup as models struggle to agreed on moisture \ncharacteristics in the sfc layer and within the DGZ, as well as the \nplacement of seeder-feeder / convergence dominant bands. As per \nusual with the first lake effect snow events of the year where \ntemperatures near the immediate lakeshores are warm, modified by \nonshore flow off the lake, the high terrain of Baraga/Marquette \ncounties will likely see the greatest snow totals by Saturday \nmorning. Local research tools investigating low level lapse rates to \ndetermine snowfall accumulation potential near the immediate \nlakeshores suggest snow will struggle to stick. Wouldn't surprise me \nto see some light snow <0.5\" in Marquette/Munising proper, but it \nmay be contingent on wet-bulb processes and if a dominant band can \nfind its way there. Latest ensembles suggest a 40-70% chance for at \nleast 4\" of snow in the Michigamme Highlands between ~Negaunee to \nHerman. Elsewhere, a trace to 2\" is likely, but there are some \nsignals of localized higher amounts in dominant band structures in \nconnection with upstream Lake Nipigon and eastern lake induced \ntroughing. These features will be fickle and hard to place until \nmore CAM guidance can sus out QPF bullseyes over the next 24-36 \nhours. NBM probability for at least 1\" of snow parks between 50-90% \nfor select areas of the Keweenaw high terrain as well as the \nPorkies/Gogebic range. Otherwise temps return near normal with highs \nin the 30s to low 40s and lows in the 20s to low 30s.",
